    "created_at": "2020-02-14T09:16:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-02-14T09:16:42Z",
    "body": "I don't think the point of rAF in a worker is to allow for worker animations or worker rendering to be combined with main-thread animation/rendering, but to side-step it entirely. I don't really see a use-case for synchronising rendering between the main thread and workers (or between different workers). The lack of a synchronisation isn't an issue in any reasonable use-case I can think of (generally, variations of displaying an animation while the main thread is busy or intermittently busy). \r\n\r\nThat said, I don't think it's completely unreasonable to mention that there isn't a promise of synchronisation - given that there's an expectation that rAF is synchronised to rendering to the screen, there may be some expectation that rAF in a worker may run basically lock-step with an uninterrupted main thread, but there are instances, even aside from the main thread being interrupted, where that might not be the case.",
