    "updated_at": "2020-02-15T03:48:30Z",
    "body": "I'm not very familiar with that part of the spec, or the implementation details, and I think one needs to make a distinction between \"rendering\", and \"running animation frame callbacks\". \r\n\r\nSo if a worker runs animation frame callbacks(see step 13 of https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/#event-loop-processing-model), then step 13.3 refers to [`updating the rendering`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/#update-the-rendering). \r\n\r\nSo when you write \"there's an expectation that rAF is synchronised to rendering to the screen\", I think you mean that running a raf is followed by potentially re-rendering, however that doesn't mean that different event-loops need to synchronize with each other. \r\n\r\nThe rendering that follows the running of frame callbacks in the worker might influence the web page as displayed to the user, but that doesn't require cooperation from the window event-loop. \r\n\r\nActually, as I understand it, the point of running frame callbacks in the worker is to enable smooth rendering even if the \"main\" window event-loop is busy with something unrelated. If the worker would have to synchronize it's frame callbacks with the window event-loop, there would seem to be little point using the worker to run those? \r\n\r\nNow if both the window, and worker, event-loops are running frame callbacks, I can imagine the UA might decide to coalesce the rendering together in some way, and that might be broadly covered in the spec by the concept of \"rendering opportunities\" https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#rendering-opportunity",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
