    "body": "> \r\n> \r\n> > Actually, as I understand it, the point of running frame callbacks in the worker is to enable smooth rendering even if the \"main\" window event-loop is busy with something unrelated. If the worker would have to synchronize it's frame callbacks with the window event-loop, there would seem to be little point using the worker to run those?\r\n> \r\n> There's no way that anything in a worker can affect what's rendered without the main thread doing work, unless I'm missing something. Workers can do offscreen canvas, but the results have to be drawn into a canvas in the main thread.\r\n> \r\n> So generally workers will do computation, but the results of that computation have to get on-screen via something (probably rAF) on the main thread.\r\n\r\nThis isn't correct - composition is asynchronous and there are already methods and scenarios where things get to the screen without involving the main thread (video, for example). I use a similar mechanism in my branch to allow this to work in WebKit - you can see that part of the work here: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=202798\r\n\r\n> If rAF in the worker has no synchronization with rAF on the main thread, then you risk phase shifting/jitter with main thread rAF which will result in dropped or doubled frames.\r\n> \r\n> I still don't get why rAF in a worker is useful at all. Just setInterval() if you want periodic work, or message from the main thread if you want to do work that ends up on the screen.\r\n\r\nIt's useful because it lets you avoid the main thread - note that this is already fully implemented in Chrome. You can see some demos here: https://devnook.github.io/OffscreenCanvasDemo/ - these demos also work in WebKit with my branch (though it's currently a bit in flux from revisions getting bits to land... I don't recall, but this branch may work: https://github.com/Cwiiis/webkit/tree/offscreen-canvas - note that the asynchronous part is only implemented for Linux though).",
