    "updated_at": "2020-02-16T05:35:16Z",
    "body": " I think this is actually worked on, discussed over at https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/3872#issuecomment-411999460\r\n\r\nMaybe @fserb can further shed light on this...\r\n\r\n> That's a feature of having OffscreenCanvas on a worker: if the main page is blocked/slow/hanging on something, the animation driven by OffscreenCanvas stays as smooth as the browser's rendering can make it. (https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/3872#issuecomment-413223645)\r\n\r\nAnd what happens if the main page is not hanging, and actually also running rafs?\r\n\r\nMy mental model, which might be completely wrong, is that assuming the rendering is at a fixed interval, and you have a window event-loop and a dedicated worker loop(or perhaps even others, what about cross-site iframes?) all running rafs, then you can have each loop run rafs at it's own rate, and then at each \"rendering interval\", you batch all the rendering side-effects of all rafs in all loops that have run until then(without needing to have loops synchronize their rafs).  \r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
